(go/var ^{:tag CmdFlags :doc "Flag holds the parsed command-line flags.\nSee ParseFlag for non-zero defaults.\n"} Flag)




(go/type CountFlag "A CountFlag is a counting integer flag.\nIt accepts -name=value to set the value directly,\nbut it also accepts -name with no =value to increment the count.\n" int)















(go/type CmdFlags "CmdFlags defines the command-line flags (see var Flag).\nEach struct field is a different flag, by default named for the lower-case of the field name.\nIf the flag name is a single letter, the default flag name is left upper-case.\nIf the flag name is \"Lower\" followed by a single letter, the default flag name is the lower-case of the last letter.\n\nIf this default flag name can't be made right, the `flag` struct tag can be used to replace it,\nbut this should be done only in exceptional circumstances: it helps everyone if the flag name\nis obvious from the field name when the flag is used elsewhere in the compiler sources.\nThe `flag:\"-\"` struct tag makes a field invisible to the flag logic and should also be used sparingly.\n\nEach field must have a `help` struct tag giving the flag help message.\n\nThe allowed field types are bool, int, string, pointers to those (for values stored elsewhere),\nCountFlag (for a counting flag), and func(string) (for a flag that uses special code for parsing).\n" (struct

